   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to the award of\ncontracts to small businesses, minority-owned business enterprises and\nwomen-owned business enterprises; and providing for the repeal of such\nprovisions upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S7960",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7960-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1678, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7960\n\nSPONSOR: RANZENHOFER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public authorities law, in\nrelation to the award of contracts to small businesses, minority-owned\nbusiness enterprises and women-owned business enterprises; and providing\nfor the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof\n\n1. \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThe bill would amend the Public Authorities Law (\"PAL\") to authorize the\nestablishment by the Dormitory Authority of the State of New York\n(\"DASNY\") of a four-year procurement set-aside pilot program (the \"Pilot\nProgram\") to further expand contracting opportunities for small busi-\nnesses (as defined in Economic Development Law (\"EDL\") § 131) and/or\nminority-owned and women-owned business enterprises (as defined in Exec-\nutive Law § 310).\n\n2.  \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe Pilot Program would create a discretionary, competitive procurement\nset-aside in an annual amount not to exceed a total value of $20 million\nor 5% of the value of all DASNY construction contracts awarded in a\ngiven fiscal year.\n\nProcurements pursuant to the Pilot Program would be undertaken in\naccordance with DASNY's procurement policy and guidelines (adopted\npursuant to PAL § 2879), with participation in the Program reserved\nexclusively to small businesses (as defined in EDL § 131) and/or minori-\nty-owned and women-owned business enterprises (as defined in Executive\nLaw § 310, notwithstanding the expiration of said section on December\n31, 2016 pursuant to L. 1988, c. 261, § 121 \nH).\n\nAllocation of procurements to the Pilot Program is to be approached\nflexibly. DASNY, in its sole discretion, must first determine which\nprocurements would be made eligible under the Pilot Program. Eligible\nprocurements must then be designated by DASNY as Pilot Program procure-\nments prior to an advertisement and request for bids or proposals. All\ndocuments used in such procurement must also indicate the Pilot Program\ndesignation.\n\nIf the total number of businesses responding to the procurement and\nconsidered capable of meeting the applicable specifications and terms is\nless than three, or if DASNY determines that acceptance will result in\nthe payment of an unreasonable price, DASNY must reject all bids and\nwithdraw the Pilot Program designation.\n\nProcurements made pursuant to PAL § 1678(28) may be undertaken in\nconjunction with and in furtherance of State Finance Law (\"SFL\") § 147\nauthorizing a mentor-protege program to foster long-term relationships\nbetween approved mentor firms and small business concerns and minority\nand women-owned businesses certified pursuant to Executive Law Article\n15-A.\n\nThe total value of contracts awarded pursuant to PAL § 1678(28) per\nfiscal year cannot exceed the greater of a total of $20 million or 5% of\nthe value of all contracts awarded by DASNY in a given fiscal year.\n\n\nThe bill would become effective immediately and provide a sunset date of\nJuly 1, 2022, for the expiration of the Pilot Program.\n\n3.  \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Pilot Program is a logical extension of recent legislative enact-\nments designed to improve opportunities in the State procurement process\nfor small, minority and women-owned businesses:\n\n-In 2009, the Legislature enacted SFL § 147 to authorize state authori-\nties and agencies to develop mentor-protege programs to foster the\ndevelopment of longterm relationships between approved mentor firms, and\nsmall business concerns and minority-owned and women-owned businesses.\n\n-In 2010, the Legislature enacted significant revisions to Executive Law\nArticle 15-A, promoting and encouraging the continued economic develop-\nment of minority-and woman-owned business enterprises participating in\nthe State procurement process.\n\n-In 2010, the Legislature also amended PAL § 2879 to provide for discre-\ntionary purchases of goods and services not exceeding $200,000 in value,\nwithout a formal competitive process, from small business concerns or\nthose certified as minority or women-owned business enterprises.\n\nThis bill will expand upon the foundation of this existing legislation\nby: (a) creating targeted, competitive procurement opportunities that\nwould be expected to further improve the success of small, minority-\nowned and women-owned businesses that contract with DASNY; and (b)\nfostering the development of alternative sources of reliable contractors\nand subcontractors ready to perform larger jobs and responsibilities\nthrough mentor-protege relationships.\n\n4. \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone.\n\n5. \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n6.  \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill shall take effect immediately and shall expire July 1, 2022,\non which date it shall be deemed repealed..\n",
